pkg:Evidence_v1_7_3 a quality:Evidence, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Gate transcript for code-abundance-rdodi v1.7.3 (PATCH release)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "PATCH: user instructed reviving the BRSF methodology from BACKLOG_ROADMAP_FRAMEWORK_STANDARD_v1_70_0.md directly rather than continuing to infer the taxonomy piecemeal from TBox/SHACL property definitions. Found a genuine structural error: the standard documents two mutually exclusive Scope<->Objective properties -- fillsScope (Objective->Scope, for a scope-first lineage) and scopeRealizesObjective (Scope->Objective, for the reverse construction order) -- and this register had used scopeRealizesObjective throughout, despite this lineage being built explicitly scope-first (independently confirmed earlier by a G13 bidirectional-traversal test). A real contradiction between the property used and the actual construction order, invisible to SHACL since both directions are independently valid vocabulary for different lineages. Fixed: removed scopeRealizesObjective, added fillsScope in the correct direction. Also checked two advisories the standard names explicitly (StageOrderWitnessShape, ScopeContentLateShape) against this register for the first time -- both fire; verified via git merge-base that the 'later commit' advisory is a known imprecision in the check (tests commit-value inequality, not chronology) and confirmed the actual commit order is correct. Read the standard's own ScopeArea/Layer/ProductScopeKind/GoalFacing sections and confirmed everything built in earlier turns already matches the documented methodology. Re-verified: pipeline order unaffected (5/5 digests reproduce), full SHACL 0 violations."@en ;
    pub:transcriptSHA256 "4450da30bad0456220cd5a4b04f9dd33782b39198991f4004b2a7eb7c6e8fdf4"^^xsd:string .
